Frontend Implementation Guide: Multi-HeadCode Drill-Down
1. UI Component Changes (The Search Dialog)
 Multi-Select Dropdown: The Head Code search input must be converted from a single-select dropdown to a multi-select dropdown (e.g., using Chips or Tags).
 Validation (Max 5): The UI must prevent the user from selecting more than 5 Head Codes. If they try to select a 6th, disable the dropdown or show a toast warning: "A maximum of 5 Head Codes can be compared simultaneously."
 Validation (Min 1): The "Search/Submit" button must be disabled until at least 1 Head Code is selected.
2. The New API Request Contract (JSON Payload)
The backend ⁠DrillDownRequest⁠ has changed. The selected Head Codes must now be sent as a root-level array called ⁠headCodes⁠, not hidden inside the ⁠filters⁠ array.
✅ Correct Initial Payload (Grid Load):






{
  "viewCode": "YSA_BANK",
  "headCodes": ["10001", "10002", "10003"], // <-- NEW MANDATORY FIELD
  "dimensions": ["HeadCode"],               // <-- MUST START WITH HeadCode
  "metrics": ["TotalAmount"],
  "filters": []
}




3. Dynamic Hierarchy Reading (Important!)
Because the backend is a metadata-driven Semantic Layer, we updated the Oracle DB so that ⁠HeadCode⁠ is now Level 10 (the root of the hierarchy).
 When the UI calls ⁠/api/analytics/config⁠ on load, the ⁠hierarchy⁠ array will now look like this: ⁠["HeadCode", "Geography", "Circle", "CGL", ...]⁠.
 The UI must blindly trust this list. The first API call it makes must pass ⁠"HeadCode"⁠ inside the ⁠dimensions⁠ array.
4. The Drill-Down State Machine (Clicking a Row)
This is the most critical logic change for the frontend state.
Scenario: The user loaded the grid for 3 Head Codes. They now click on the row for Head Code ⁠"10001"⁠ to drill down into its Geography.
Frontend Action Required: 1. Keep the global ⁠headCodes⁠ array exactly the same.
2. Advance the ⁠dimensions⁠ array to the next level from the config (⁠["Geography"]⁠).
3. Push the clicked Head Code into the ⁠filters⁠ array so the backend knows which specific row the user clicked.
